        "intro": "The following is adapted from a resident's letter to a planning commission regarding a proposed change allowing duplexes in single-family zones.",
        "text": "The commission should approve this rezoning proposal. Three separate studies from comparable mid-sized cities found that allowing duplex construction in formerly single-family zones increased overall housing supply by 8 to 14 percent within five years, without measurably increasing traffic congestion or straining local schools beyond capacity. Critics of this proposal argue it will fundamentally change neighborhood character, and I understand why that concern feels significant — nobody wants their block to become unrecognizable. But 'character' is not the same as 'zoning category,' and a neighborhood of tree-lined streets and front porches can accommodate a duplex just as easily as it accommodates the single-family homes already there. The actual choice before this commission is not preservation versus disruption. It is whether more families get to live somewhere near their jobs and schools, or whether they get pushed further out, adding to the very traffic critics claim to be worried about."
