        "intro": "The following is adapted from a science journalist's critique of health reporting.",
        "text": "The headline read: 'New Study Finds Coffee Drinkers Live Longer.' The actual study, buried three paragraphs into most articles that covered it, followed twelve hundred adults for six years and found a correlation between coffee consumption and longevity — a correlation the study's own authors explicitly noted could be explained by any number of confounding factors, including that people healthy enough to enjoy coffee regularly may simply already be healthier than people who cannot. None of the articles I found bothered to explain the difference between correlation and causation to their readers, and I suspect this is not an accident. 'Coffee is correlated with an outcome that has several possible causes, one of which might be coffee' does not sell nearly as many clicks as 'Coffee Drinkers Live Longer' does."
