        "title": "On an Organization in Decline",
        "intro": "The following is adapted from a nonprofit board member's internal memo.",
        "text": "This organization used to be a river. Ideas moved through it, found their own channels, occasionally flooded a bank and reshaped the landscape around them, and always, eventually, found their way to the sea. Somewhere in the last five years, we built a dam. I don't think anyone intended to. Each individual approval process, each additional sign-off, each new committee formed to review the committee before it, seemed reasonable in isolation. But a river dammed one small barrier at a time is still, eventually, a lake — still water, going nowhere, reflecting back only what's already standing on its own shore. We need to ask ourselves honestly: are we managing risk, or are we just watching the water rise behind a wall we built ourselves, one reasonable brick at a time?"
