        "text": "The house was smaller than I remembered, which everyone warned me it would be, and which I did not believe until I stood in the kitchen and could touch both counters without moving my feet. I had built this room, in memory, into something closer to a cathedral. It is, in fact, a room. Just a room, with water stains on the ceiling that were there when we lived here and are, I'm somewhat relieved to report, still exactly where I left them.\n\nThere is a particular kind of grief in discovering that a place you carried inside you for twenty years was never as large as the carrying made it feel. But there is also, if you sit with it long enough, something almost generous in the correction. The real kitchen, the small one, the one where my mother actually stood — that kitchen gets to exist now, alongside the cathedral I'd been keeping in its place. Both are true. I'm choosing, today, to be glad of that, though I reserve the right to change my mind about it by dinner."
